{
  "image_name": "1f4101e6c489955581650934f524fe95.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how damaged mitochondria can trigger innate immune signaling through the release of mitochondrial nucleic acids. A stressed mitochondrion contains mtDNA, and the diagram shows that when protective quality-control pathways such as mitophagy are limited, mtDNA can escape through membrane permeabilization routes involving BAK–BAX, MOMP, and mPTP/VDAC1. PINK1 and Parkin are depicted as regulators near the mitochondrial surface, while PNPase appears to restrain nucleic acid release.\n\nOnce outside, mitochondrial DNA or DNA-containing nucleoids activate several sensing pathways. One branch leads to TLR9, which signals through MYD88 to NF-κB. Another major branch shows cytosolic DNA engaging cGAS, which activates STING and then TBK1, leading to phosphorylation of IRF3 and inflammatory gene expression. Smaller mtDNA fragments are also routed into the cGAS pathway. In parallel, mitochondrial RNA-like species released from the organelle are sensed by MDA5 and PKR.\n\nAll of these pathways converge on nuclear transcriptional responses, producing type I interferon and inflammatory cytokines. Overall, the figure conveys that mitochondrial damage can convert normally contained mitochondrial nucleic acids into potent danger signals that drive inflammation.",
